+    "tests_flatten_batch_identity": "that the venue's own submit time is what `time.time()` reads just before submit() (it is OUR clock and the row names it `venue_submit_call`, not the venue's), that historical rows can be repaired (they cannot: the ledger is append-only and the lead's constraint is that they stay as written — a reader needing true times for history must derive them from fills and venue records and name that source), that the real 2026-09-12 batch is in this tree (it is not; the clone's committed state holds 2026-08-01 only, and the 09-12 measurement is devices/new01_flatten_identity_real_batches.py: 255 rows, 0 sharing a key with their readback, judged=False / NO_READBACK, 255/255 rows with submit_ts 0.135-141.950 s after their own fill), or anything about fills recovery or attempt_idx (NEW-02, FX-EXEC's item). It proves, on the REAL committed 2026-08-01 batch read-only and on batches written into tmp, that the historical batch carries one anchor_ts and one submit_ts, does not share a key with its readback, reports an execution preceding its own submission on every filled row, and is recorded by §4-5e as NO_READBACK — and that a batch written by the fixed writer shares ONE key with its readback (derived from the batch's own name, equal by construction rather than by a caller passing the same value), is JUDGED by §4-5e for the first time, carries the broker's real submit moment with `submit_ts_source=venue_submit_call`, names the row-write fallback instead of hiding it, keeps `submit_ts is None` meaning exactly one thing with no source column, keeps `read_ts` as the real read moment distinct from the batch key, leaves a caller that passes no key on the previous behaviour, and does NOT drag in EXE-01's LOCAL response (whose batch holds the rest of the book on purpose and whose readback is the whole remaining account — judging it against intent ZERO would trip the whole-book ladder one evaluation later). Both production call sites and the real broke