+    "tests_cond4_amended_transfer_day": "that the amendment records are themselves correct (FX-EXEC2 owns the builder and its positive control: the 8 post-fix rows' own per-asset split equals the recomputation 8/8; this side verified their sha integrity — 250/250 row_sha256 reproduce from the live daily_nav lines — and nothing about their arithmetic), that the live tree HAS the records (it does not: FIXPROGRAM §10 deferred every write-back, so every pre-fix transfer day will name itself until the apply step runs, which is the ruling's own prescription), or whether a POST-FIX transfer day should be priced from its USDT-only slice instead of the carrier sum (no such day exists yet; the slice is reported as `row_usdt_slice` and deliberately NOT used, because that is a second change to a trigger and needs its own ruling). It proves, on pilot_log trees built in tmp with one mode root each, that a pre-fix TRANSFER day with a valid record is priced from `amended.realised_pnl_usdt` and named in `days_amended` with the record's income sha and per-day USDT delta; that all five ways a record fails to cover a row — absent file, row_sha256 mismatch against the ledger line, an unparsable line, a duplicate key with different content (dropped, never resolved by position), and a non-finite amended total — leave the RECORDED value in place and name the day `unamended_prefix_day` with a reason; that none of the five sets `blind` or breaks the drawdown chain; that a post-fix row and an ordinary (non-transfer) day are priced exactly as before and an ordinary day never consults a record at all; and that exactly ONE definition of the amendment loader exists in live/ ops/ scheduler/ — a MERGE GATE that goes red when FX-EXEC2's branch is stacked without replacing its own copy with an import. The real-data reproduction is a device, not a cell: devices/led04_cond4_watchdog_reproduction.py shows the fixed watchdog reproducing the live last_eval value bit for bit with no records (1.1704) and moving it by 0.2617-0.2680 pp with the real 250 records, against FX-EXEC2's independently measured 0.2613 pp.",
+    "tests_offschedule_held_book": "whether an off-schedule run should be able to happen at all (that is gate 0c's ruling and is unchanged), that 1% of gross is the right flat-intent limit (policy, unchanged), or anything about the venue. It proves, on the REAL 2026-08-01 06:29Z off-schedule LIVE anchor's own rows — its `anchors` row, its 109 never-submitted order rows — re-keyed to sit an hour after that day's 20:00Z anchor and holding that anchor's real book, that WITHOUT a halt_kind the anchor is judged against intent ZERO: `halted_intent_flat`, gate `flat_intent_legacy`, a 96.39% deviation against the 1% limit, §4-5e triggered, no name scope, and the ladder flattens all 105 names with reduce-only — which is what every off-schedule run would do on ef60f85 with a book on; and that WITH `halt_kind=off_schedule` the same rows are judged as a held book against the target vector and nothing trips. Reachability is measured, not assumed: devices/probe_i6_offschedule.py over the live anchor_runs.log (999 runs) finds 570 DRY_RUN, 2 TESTNET and 3 LIVE off-schedule runs, and both LIVE ones that reached phase_C were CLEAN only because their book was empty (readback gross 0.00, dev_frac 0.0). It also proves the writer: finalize_anchor marks the row on an off-schedule halt and NOT when the run's action is FLATTEN (the staleness ladder is emptying the book on purpose, where intent ZERO is correct), a trip halt's row still carries no marker, an ordinary anchor's row is unchanged, and the three held-book kinds live in one list in the judge.",
+    "tests_book_observability": "that an anchor which died before writing anything is detectable (it leaves no row of any kind — the 2026-09-09 12Z anchor is absent from the record entirely; \"did an anchor run at all\" is the off-box healthchecks deadman ping's question, and a second implementation of it belongs nowhere near the watchdog), that a TRUNCATED readback should or should not halt opening (it does not: it is named PARTIAL and paged, and whether a short write deserves a halt is a ruling nobody has taken), that 5b's comparison domain covers the names a truncated write dropped (it does not; only 5e counts them, through coverage), or anything about the venue. It proves, on the REAL committed day state/live/pilot_log/20260801 re-shaped in tmp and on a MockBroker holding that day's real book, that deleting the newest scheduled anchor's position_readback rows — which on ef60f85 silently turns that day's genuine §4-5e BREAK into CLEAN judged on the previous anchor — now leaves §4-5b, §4-5e and §4-7 each naming UNOBSERVED and blind, in conditions_blind (so run_anchor pages and resume_from_trip refuses), with NOT ONE order sent: no trip, no local response, no reduce-only key; that the ladder's own protective-flatten batch (an anchor key with orders and no readback, 10 of 10 on the live record, its readback 0.116-0.290 s later under a separate key) does NOT make anything blind, because the reference is the newest `anchors` ROW as rebalance_id.py requires; that the real 2026-08-29 HOLD-anchor shape and a tree that never observes the book (DRY_RUN) are both un-halted and distinguished from a lost book; that a real §4-5a outage on the very tree that is blind still trips and still flattens; that finalize_anchor names WHICH of the four causes wrote no rows (READ_FAILED / NO_ACCOUNT / OBSERVED_EMPTY / WRITE_TRUNCATED), which the log alone cannot distinguish, and that run_anchor hands that verdict to the watchdog; and that anchor_loop.book_unobserved_halt halts opening on