+    "tests_cond2_judged_text": "that any reader of the §4-2 record or trigger text acts correctly on it (none in this repo parses either; guard_twin outside the repo is not checked). It proves, on pilot_log trees built in tmp, that the trigger prints the MOST RECENT priced day's loss and names that day while an older, worse day stays in the window (the window's minimum is no longer printed as the tripping number), that judged_on is rendered from recent_day on a tripping, a quiet and a no-priced-day window, and that watchdog.py carries no hard-coded date in that wording.",
+    "tests_proportional_response": "that 2% of gross and 5 names are the right lines (policy set after the 09-12 incident, frozen by R-14), that the venue behaves for reduce-only market exits during -4400 or price protection as it does in MockBroker, that the off-box page is DELIVERED (run_anchor's call is pinned statically; delivery is the anchor's receipt), and that ops/resume_from_trip.sh and ops/unseed_rehearsal_halt.py refuse end to end (both run a public network probe; their refusal branches are pinned statically and the EMA keep/remove snippet is executed verbatim). It proves, through watchdog.run on a held 100-name MockBroker book and on the real 2026-09-12 12Z rows re-shaped so (a)(b) keeps the contradiction, that a name-scoped trigger set (§4-5b, §4-5e split_unauth per-name clause alone, §4-7 drift) of <= 5 names whose doubt is <= 2% of the anchors row's target gross gets a LOCAL response — opening halted, resting orders cancelled and the WHOLE venue position flattened reduce-only for THOSE names only, the reduce-only key not set, state kind proportional_local — at 4/5/6 names and 1,999/2,000/2,001 USDT of 100,000, across the §4-5b/§4-5e union; that any book-level trigger beside it (§4-2, §4-5a) and the negative controls alone (§4-2, §4-4b, §4-5a, §4-5c) still flatten every name; that an unknown doubt or gross is named and never becomes a book-level flatten on its own while a KNOWN part over 2% still does; that a trigger nobody scoped is book-level; that the doubt of a name is a sum over every leg of unknown size (review C9: row order cannot change the route); that a local response never overwrites a standing trip's record and does not reset the harvest EMA; that the config switch false restores the ladder and an unreadable config reads ON; that unreadable positions across the book never reached the ladder before this change and still do not; and that the 2026-09-09 receipt's name sets (9 + 52) stay on the ladder.",
     "tests_income_twin_rows": "that the venue keys income rows the way the fixture does — it proves, against an in-process fake of /fapi/v1/income, that a trade's COMMISSION and REALIZED_PNL twins (same tranId) are BOTH summed, that a row re-served across a page boundary is still counted once, and that the old tranId-only key would have dropped exactly one twin per trade (positive control). Whether the venue also shares tranIds across OTHER pairs of types, or emits a third row per trade in some product, is a fact about the venue that only the live ledger reconciliation (income vs fills) can keep proving.",
     "tests_transport_resilience": "that the VENUE will be reachable on the resend — it proves, against an in-process fake of urlopen, that a request-phase failure on GET/DELETE is resent a bounded number of times while an order POST is NEVER resent under any failure — transport in either phase, HTTP 5xx, 200 non-JSON — but settled only by the venue's own order record (found ⇒ adopted, -2013 twice ⇒ recorded as not sent, else ambiguous), that the requote carries ambiguity as rested and the top-up keeps a confirmed chunk and writes UNKNOWN never zero, that a still-unresolvable POST is carried as possibly-resting, that the executor records transport rows and trips its circuit breaker, and that an exception escaping the submit loop cancels what rested, writes rows and pages before re-raising. It cannot see whether the venue actually processed a request whose answer we lost when the query ALSO fails (that order is handed to phase B as possibly-resting and to the next sweep), nor whether a 0.5 s pause is long enough for the venue to have decided before we ask it, nor whether Telegram delivers the page",
